near transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $[T]_{B}=[T]_{C}^{-1}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}$\n- **II.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{C}=[T]_{B}^{-1}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/2e/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049895 :topic/id #uuid "1c611a71-aaa1-5a41-a6cd-aa83bbba97af" :topic/math-academy-id 3645 :topic/title "The Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dbfc974-64a1-5ba8-a98a-3c44ced3256a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452317} {:answer/id #uuid "4692bc9a-0874-5399-a418-98fb85a5cec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dbfc974-64a1-5ba8-a98a-3c44ced3256a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452317} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e47d84-f057-5e0a-ae70-ec0d33904e89" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453006} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420fa2b5-f5a7-5d27-bc3d-9bbf0b0cd31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452319} {:answer/id #uuid "27f8b042-1a66-5c50-9a92-c6c664837fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27f8b042-1a66-5c50-9a92-c6c664837fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452320} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e76e66-d34f-5c65-9093-a22e5839e130" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7d6839f-b647-50f5-9788-3ee7b2fa1f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452321} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4185c8-8f28-5d02-aeb6-193573874b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7d6839f-b647-50f5-9788-3ee7b2fa1f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452321} :answer-field/id #uuid "883ab182-da38-5ef9-bdd1-6c14d39cea71" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2667e6b9-8784-550d-a849-0ee298d78d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-97078" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/title "Linear Algebra: Placement Exam" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045462 :db/ident :activity.type/diagnostic} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549302} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550037}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9e07b5-9f0c-5f9e-b5e0-dd0bb5ddbb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452329} {:answer/id #uuid "d14bb30d-305a-547d-9203-a605f0933196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9e07b5-9f0c-5f9e-b5e0-dd0bb5ddbb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452329} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e160ff3-065c-5657-8ce0-3c4b6d039613" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "818a42fb-6b7f-5df4-8ab0-b6fbd9ce9828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452331} {:answer/id #uuid "8e28d75e-6501-56ba-b2ec-7c348ccf4ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "818a42fb-6b7f-5df4-8ab0-b6fbd9ce9828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452331} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02af0b4-2c7a-5f06-8bce-9c46c66d51fc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c808587e-3884-59ef-af9c-8252842bfe9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452333} {:answer/id #uuid "798caed6-3130-5378-93c1-4a0623b4f841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "798caed6-3130-5378-93c1-4a0623b4f841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452334} :answer-field/id #uuid "f055b1e5-126e-5f0a-bbc9-b574c36ec312" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b24ab75-1f17-54d0-bd60-01447f2e1769" :question/math-academy-id "q-97149" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **II.** $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **III.** $⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $a=0$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac261e67-ef83-599b-9f7a-2f0221d53c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452335} {:answer/id #uuid "a8955430-a9ed-5bcb-ac12-138c73b1312a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac261e67-ef83-599b-9f7a-2f0221d53c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452335} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ce4c66-39b4-5114-9f3a-f7a49fb4749a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4385f63-a19e-59b3-b375-1f827b7a2b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452337} {:answer/id #uuid "f93fb5fe-2a57-5b9f-8207-21687f865974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93fb5fe-2a57-5b9f-8207-21687f865974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452338} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e2913a5-5b90-507c-ba81-c9f768ab74f3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1d2b1a3-de50-5a4d-8436-402d7d7ff9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452339} {:answer/id #uuid "80eb1877-a06a-52e2-9a0b-c6ad7075ac0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1d2b1a3-de50-5a4d-8436-402d7d7ff9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452339} :answer-field/id #uuid "efec9971-12d9-5dac-9de5-e52ca41b457f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea5645c8-5c4c-53e6-a126-c5a12be2f712" :question/math-academy-id "q-97229" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **II.** $⟨ia,ia⟩=⟨a,a⟩$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **III.** $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument we have\n\n$$\n⟨ia,ia⟩=(i\\cdot \\overset{¯}{i})⟨a,a⟩=-{i}^{2}⟨a,a⟩=⟨a,a⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number, in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"} :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548355} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549116}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180f3b65-b2cb-53f6-8c04-72897f825aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452341} {:answer/id #uuid "ca765503-72af-5a62-90a8-9c3ea9e4d41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "180f3b65-b2cb-53f6-8c04-72897f825aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452341} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9da039e-c54e-5155-a392-e4ef2ae3f67c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453018} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2fb392-f964-5fb5-8969-a83bb0cb21b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452343} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb82dcb-5e7a-5feb-81a2-992930935c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c2fb392-f964-5fb5-8969-a83bb0cb21b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452343} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8240300-118e-5033-a435-f2278fe983d5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453019} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6607ad8c-6b9d-5cd8-a588-8f899222623c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452345} {:answer/id #uuid "a6876d72-3057-5616-b5e0-069e3f6bc457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6876d72-3057-5616-b5e0-069e3f6bc457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452346} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d2a334-c96f-58a4-8af6-4b6abbb4289e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad8bf150-2b71-5d8f-8288-e353f02a776f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97248" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **II.** $⟨a,-ia⟩=-i⟨a,a⟩$ for any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **III.** $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the antilinearity in the second argument we have\n\n$$\n⟨a,-ia⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\ne -i⟨a,a⟩\n$$\n\n \nfor any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "914c5cc0-77de-5e5a-9430-0c8663b7fb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452347} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5b3124-7a98-5669-a577-2b30cc503898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e5b3124-7a98-5669-a577-2b30cc503898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452348} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dc138d2-4f7c-59f8-a707-746570e3a3b8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5971c655-545f-582e-9080-64ecfc5176a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452349} {:answer/id #uuid "aae144d6-fa3b-515c-a5b3-99f9a33f4896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5971c655-545f-582e-9080-64ecfc5176a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452349} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bcc5079-bb62-5377-8955-c42eea6e68ea" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453022} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5ada01-d7b5-56ef-a0ba-92bb91518486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452351} {:answer/id #uuid "83a11f7d-c0f2-5e90-97d1-c0f4da2784c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e5ada01-d7b5-56ef-a0ba-92bb91518486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452351} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94f1c25-e6f5-54ad-badc-528ff35eb492" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fac0a4e-d3e8-507c-b2ff-dcb288cf0e43" :question/math-academy-id "q-97258" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n\n- **II.** $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n\n- **III.** $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"} :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445240} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445241}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3859bb5-c8b9-50e8-8eef-3af0c8937ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452353} {:answer/id #uuid "da6c7dee-677d-5806-85b1-6f0588358ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da6c7dee-677d-5806-85b1-6f0588358ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452354} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca96214-aff5-5f05-ada1-12ce791f90fc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453024} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d6b082d-2576-5e1d-8655-c6e913dd6e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452355} {:answer/id #uuid "44335011-eb8f-5a0b-bb95-f537858c1e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44335011-eb8f-5a0b-bb95-f537858c1e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452356} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9df55b-6d6a-5739-9911-e251543e8b83" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edba4c44-368b-51d4-98cb-48bf0ea196b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452357} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbde816-1bd9-5b75-8fc3-21d0b2f05e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edba4c44-368b-51d4-98cb-48bf0ea196b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452357} :answer-field/id #uuid "7767b767-280a-5089-ad8f-b2a80f87660b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d89a9f6-d33c-56fa-8ee8-b25b137af49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97264" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **II.** $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- **III.** $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"} :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530864} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531678}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179 :topic/id #uuid "8633737d-b07f-5466-99f0-a422586920c4" :topic/math-academy-id 3351 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0006d664-b8ff-5634-986f-5c0d4e1d7dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469884} {:answer/id #uuid "83c6dacd-fb1e-5471-803a-abd44bac5e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0006d664-b8ff-5634-986f-5c0d4e1d7dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469884} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebde2a3-3507-5638-9b44-f686205ba48d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470554} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d127a6-6075-5768-b2dc-3a7b506eee92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469886} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbf38e8-b177-50e1-9fb8-9e14988ed9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dbf38e8-b177-50e1-9fb8-9e14988ed9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469887} :answer-field/id #uuid "318d7144-ff77-5eb1-a82b-9cbffe6faa92" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470555} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179c1b11-36a5-5f98-920b-76f389dbee6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469888} {:answer/id #uuid "d13d24a9-2960-5539-8b04-298e83c2a041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "179c1b11-36a5-5f98-920b-76f389dbee6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469888} :answer-field/id #uuid "403154ae-9845-5bc7-b450-f3877f734072" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cae22cc3-a67e-53bb-bb36-eebf3af162ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-97288" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- **II.** At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- **III.** At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-2+1=-1<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-1+1=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=1+1=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa69f6116eba6462c4fe18f2e535d83db350f0797cf1fcd895ac6df9a4d6fd7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179 :topic/id #uuid "8633737d-b07f-5466-99f0-a422586920c4" :topic/math-academy-id 3351 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c98c4e-5e6c-5db9-9709-30890569a443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452359} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3c146b-f631-50f2-9adb-9d61f83920f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac3c146b-f631-50f2-9adb-9d61f83920f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452360} :answer-field/id #uuid "daff269f-d1d1-5289-befe-b825d12977f3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "403709a9-fc37-5554-8fda-4d6f19f04cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452361} {:answer/id #uuid "ab28e99f-97f5-5181-a4a0-405504e518f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab28e99f-97f5-5181-a4a0-405504e518f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452362} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ed9b4c-c797-5042-8f2e-9a28f60ee73b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453028} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ccff2a4-3b73-554e-b45f-64486b77a031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452363} {:answer/id #uuid "1c35c4e9-abab-5547-92ec-9dd9fc0194c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c35c4e9-abab-5547-92ec-9dd9fc0194c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452364} :answer-field/id #uuid "a923ebc2-6d02-50c9-9b2c-abdaaab1e550" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6350e51-d7f7-5f85-8d67-4cf2337fbb2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97289" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- **II.** At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- **III.** At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=2(-1)=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=2(0)=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=2(1)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69065d8e064b0fd679f059925f5445dcb2c7d037f180d3a144ee527ea408e8db.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179 :topic/id #uuid "8633737d-b07f-5466-99f0-a422586920c4" :topic/math-academy-id 3351 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fe2147c-1f86-5070-82c6-693a696e1103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452365} {:answer/id #uuid "8130913a-e4b9-5961-8bb2-4dc3778284d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fe2147c-1f86-5070-82c6-693a696e1103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452365} :answer-field/id #uuid "74430b21-a196-5035-918b-75715c7db7e3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453030} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb799ce8-a4b6-514e-8a11-ae7660dd6556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452367} {:answer/id #uuid "01da4839-d736-5a6a-96fa-bbd093b5e0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01da4839-d736-5a6a-96fa-bbd093b5e0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452368} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7a140d-7cac-55ba-b161-15343ea5e8af" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453031} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9362a2a-195c-51c4-9dcb-4faea3cf0a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452369} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e47bdf-4d9d-5986-9ca5-4bdcc1dfc043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3e47bdf-4d9d-5986-9ca5-4bdcc1dfc043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452370} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d36bfd-8e60-5cdf-b670-ef9621ab7113" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fed752d-147f-589f-b531-b8f9d3875529" :question/math-academy-id "q-97291" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- **II.** At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- **III.** At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=(-2)^{2}=4>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $4$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=(0)^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=(1)^{2}=1>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $1$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c6e2e486ae770bc99ef9fe41f02d224de65119ade910d303ad3f57f12e8d84b.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179 :topic/id #uuid "8633737d-b07f-5466-99f0-a422586920c4" :topic/math-academy-id 3351 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154f9c55-fa06-5edc-aa18-dcf55a6ec314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452371} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a78ed1-2956-5b34-8509-13f418923165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9a78ed1-2956-5b34-8509-13f418923165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452372} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1648c6f-4f1e-5ed9-aa05-a613a04e751a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453033} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fc85e73-9df1-57c0-bd7b-389d4617ae8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452373} {:answer/id #uuid "f734c67f-ee97-507e-b57a-0c8c7f7496f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f734c67f-ee97-507e-b57a-0c8c7f7496f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452374} :answer-field/id #uuid "222d103d-37b8-52b9-b140-a84819683bf4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec0ed88a-36c1-5f7c-a9a1-7f904f629949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452375} {:answer/id #uuid "450a5377-249f-51c2-b1c4-d8e425a5860d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "450a5377-249f-51c2-b1c4-d8e425a5860d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452376} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ea99e78-c93a-57d3-af14-62e1ba23e473" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c547f26-e43a-5839-bf5f-d8b1c5da69a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97292" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n- **II.** At every point along the line $x=1,$ the slope of the slope field varies\n- **III.** At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-(-1)^{2}=-1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=-{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(1,y)$ depends on $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-(0)^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/272427db87d74e3d4ae09dc69b7add53e8def9ead184a1ab90afc99fc0a7628e.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be7f699-9974-5201-8e47-accce63d5602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452377} {:answer/id #uuid "19455cdc-333a-5561-bab6-00ec79218d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be7f699-9974-5201-8e47-accce63d5602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452377} :answer-field/id #uuid "5582b199-c5bf-5709-9377-1286b2173eaa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d491e753-0d53-5704-b768-2cde723d0d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452379} {:answer/id #uuid "fda07ff9-ba7b-5689-857e-ab9f1cd84952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda07ff9-ba7b-5689-857e-ab9f1cd84952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452380} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3df9eb2-97cb-5280-9fe0-ea7eec6cc287" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d1794a0-b828-5e06-b8cc-54a54ad72949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452381} {:answer/id #uuid "b15deefa-87b8-5a6a-89b0-4f57df04b649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d1794a0-b828-5e06-b8cc-54a54ad72949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452381} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b86e18f-b4ac-528d-b5e3-1bc836241d7d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "354bca31-ea8a-5e1d-a896-d3e34e677d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-97296" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n\n- **II.** $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n\n- **III.** There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"} :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445240} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445244} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"} :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530864} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531713}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc8dac23-f647-5deb-86a4-621fd31c1d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452383} {:answer/id #uuid "6e03bff6-a5ff-50e6-9218-131abe220023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e03bff6-a5ff-50e6-9218-131abe220023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452384} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9c2f28-e2b4-59be-ae0d-5cc27032c71a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d384c3-9e31-5d82-9ef2-635c223f8ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452385} {:answer/id #uuid "140655d4-6272-5a39-a781-b9c1417ede63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30d384c3-9e31-5d82-9ef2-635c223f8ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452385} :answer-field/id #uuid "e50118f5-3a96-5d52-9890-18e710ce477d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1709b99a-c168-519e-a774-35d4b3d2341e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452387} {:answer/id #uuid "f478c422-93f8-5410-a67c-fea2ce11d298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f478c422-93f8-5410-a67c-fea2ce11d298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452388} :answer-field/id #uuid "86902f25-2bcd-545b-9255-65d88893b5e5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8247f7d2-00cd-54b9-9bd1-43c13383de3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97303" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨b,a⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **II.** $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨-ia,b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **III.** $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. A complex  inner product is linear  in the first argument,  so we have \n\n$$\n⟨-ia,b⟩=-i⟨a,b⟩\\ne i⟨a,b⟩,\n$$\n\nin general.\n\n- Statement III is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"} :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530864} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531711}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097 :topic/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417cc2f7-2cea-5426-a65d-1130f570f37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452389} {:answer/id #uuid "77908b85-72d6-598e-8c8c-8ec557330a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417cc2f7-2cea-5426-a65d-1130f570f37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452389} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c983e51-246d-505c-80b2-50e05fb4520a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453042} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7271070-c7f2-54f7-8de9-679fd1b74137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452391} {:answer/id #uuid "1444eec8-5b27-5850-b3bd-b85b02906558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1444eec8-5b27-5850-b3bd-b85b02906558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452392} :answer-field/id #uuid "57c4a420-a297-5930-8e9b-06ea5a054b71" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afea3ce6-6a9f-582e-b5b3-d9aef7133b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452393} {:answer/id #uuid "c1066333-531a-58c7-916d-9fd634978a42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afea3ce6-6a9f-582e-b5b3-d9aef7133b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452393} :answer-field/id #uuid "71913576-27b7-510d-b8b5-58432fc3e668" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d0792af-c54b-5167-a6cf-da7598a4b23d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97306" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,-ia⟩=-i⟨a,a⟩$ for any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **II.** $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **III.** $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.  By the antilinearity of $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$  in the second argument we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ia⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\ne -i⟨a,a⟩\n$$\n\n \nfor any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}.$\n\n- Statement II  is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b21fe66-8492-5d17-97c0-fee28d852f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452395} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cc7c45-679e-5c92-85eb-fdc51e826da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3cc7c45-679e-5c92-85eb-fdc51e826da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452396} :answer-field/id #uuid "51873899-664d-5819-a145-3f3fecc5c83f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453045} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dca37905-a856-50d2-91e4-25768462720f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452397} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6a9579-4874-5317-ad50-5bd45835eb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dca37905-a856-50d2-91e4-25768462720f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452397} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf568a4-f601-5390-b9a6-5df7696a20d1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f174f582-c296-57fb-90e9-5cc880809de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452399} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c75e7a-9c06-5da5-a06b-4e1815e7d645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0c75e7a-9c06-5da5-a06b-4e1815e7d645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452400} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e08c26-c3df-5f51-a440-819a7bbba1f5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d17c886-492c-5ed0-9034-9494d7fd5daf" :question/math-academy-id "q-97443" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1))-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d909dc-b9cc-58f6-bb2b-25a8f80e1941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452401} {:answer/id #uuid "2392b706-0679-5870-b8f1-88b55f8a640d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2392b706-0679-5870-b8f1-88b55f8a640d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452402} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0f4ea39-f39e-545b-94b4-162cea75b15f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453048} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aba97a89-96b2-5568-a6b4-321f97061c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452403} {:answer/id #uuid "782bd1a7-a489-5094-8b91-606fca371e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aba97a89-96b2-5568-a6b4-321f97061c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452403} :answer-field/id #uuid "58be794a-9c14-5e0c-9b19-1744a4067766" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c414cf-054f-5450-8d89-dbd581b1d6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452405} {:answer/id #uuid "82d29fd7-af00-51ac-979f-dc8f9f13b5d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3c414cf-054f-5450-8d89-dbd581b1d6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452405} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35cb758-1173-57e7-b791-5963fbd2496d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65fcd4f1-eb38-5308-baf7-7bd3106cdba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97445" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,3]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,3],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)={e}^{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2{e}^{3}+1$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=1$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{e}^{3}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3}-({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e3a6567-add1-5ac2-9492-9b3911c4e0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452407} {:answer/id #uuid "24cdd274-bb6d-5ed3-a90e-ef4b381ae89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24cdd274-bb6d-5ed3-a90e-ef4b381ae89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452408} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2602bbf-6f5c-5bef-8d30-29b248c29764" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2c72fe-ad32-5ccd-9202-558deff20196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452409} {:answer/id #uuid "3d45eeb2-34bd-5f17-9b64-a588aa2442ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2c72fe-ad32-5ccd-9202-558deff20196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452409} :answer-field/id #uuid "8acac60b-2493-53f4-844a-9f82af58d6b9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb802222-f878-52f8-b931-8e6aa95957fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452411} {:answer/id #uuid "b7260ef6-a77d-5a66-9e00-8c5dabe3c051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb802222-f878-52f8-b931-8e6aa95957fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452411} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb59a9c8-6c71-559c-8e74-fd29b03c4f5f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c591f6d1-f8d9-5efb-9959-52acee26e120" :question/math-academy-id "q-97448" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1-{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=-2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3/2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1032df2a-f8af-50fb-bb60-32e1fbb1af15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452413} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6a2f02-78bb-5193-a858-1aa5b606252c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1032df2a-f8af-50fb-bb60-32e1fbb1af15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452413} :answer-field/id #uuid "48bc6f7b-8a82-5d82-9f4e-70afc162dbdc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b71135b1-b085-5c32-8a23-43a663474b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452415} {:answer/id #uuid "02b6bc90-c2db-5bf9-aaaf-b53e23eaf104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02b6bc90-c2db-5bf9-aaaf-b53e23eaf104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452416} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f4b4ed3-1b90-5d47-b391-ee5dbbf141d1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be11b02a-310f-5aa3-909c-b8c58993622c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452417} {:answer/id #uuid "284abb42-c669-5d39-8de9-145b358632f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "284abb42-c669-5d39-8de9-145b358632f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452418} :answer-field/id #uuid "0691d9bf-8dc8-5016-8c72-f67a4b6d362c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f2adb07-0219-5290-bbb5-6e66128c62bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-97449" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ and $g(t)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}{2}|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ba28d4d-62f6-5ab0-9502-85dc2bfb9cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452419} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd26d9b-af6b-523c-89af-5533f38401fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ba28d4d-62f6-5ab0-9502-85dc2bfb9cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452419} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6a55637-e67c-58dd-9db5-70f22596e9c3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23502feb-a170-544a-a725-bbccb2a2f2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452421} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa6cce0-fa16-5915-849c-426fb302a206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23502feb-a170-544a-a725-bbccb2a2f2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452421} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3dd7665-1aaa-5df3-b589-7b04d06e2b67" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a99d62e6-4e7d-5f91-8bf3-eb77fad6e263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452423} {:answer/id #uuid "2d925e50-7850-5f07-8db6-ba284e4a2bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d925e50-7850-5f07-8db6-ba284e4a2bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452424} :answer-field/id #uuid "46142dab-d3b3-5273-a62f-da3826c54e1a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5f9b456-0ac1-5b4f-9e54-6e0e7b4c5409" :question/math-academy-id "q-97450" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )}{6}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9639d9f6-5fc6-5649-9d79-dfb677c58547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452425} {:answer/id #uuid "59dd406c-6dae-56bd-8629-711d79613c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9639d9f6-5fc6-5649-9d79-dfb677c58547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452425} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a249449-db53-5ccb-94a6-3060faa5b0a5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d71c0a-e5b5-5371-ba03-225fa31b5188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452427} {:answer/id #uuid "6a963d19-f4cf-50ee-aaec-c77a41bf04d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a963d19-f4cf-50ee-aaec-c77a41bf04d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452428} :answer-field/id #uuid "8161f941-02df-588e-9f7e-7a593219a5c1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f892e29-b44f-5f63-bba5-28f968957e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452429} {:answer/id #uuid "39c03eb7-b08f-5cc6-a0a7-a164b982ff15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f892e29-b44f-5f63-bba5-28f968957e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452429} :answer-field/id #uuid "52103090-e08b-575c-8624-dcdfeb5d80e9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7e7515b-c5f0-52cd-b9cf-1d4546466e79" :question/math-academy-id "q-97452" :question/problem "Let $V=C[1,{e}^{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[1,{e}^{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\frac{1}{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95bf5c87-5d3a-5cc2-9253-891ef7730177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438628} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5d5215-469a-5799-ba24-eb8bc4c358d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95bf5c87-5d3a-5cc2-9253-891ef7730177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438628} :answer-field/id #uuid "f545d324-f9c3-507e-983b-fd1001340ff1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b8f3b3-189f-5b75-9ccc-ddd834e75d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438630} {:answer/id #uuid "d83106e2-c95a-541f-8d33-b2ba054c50ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83106e2-c95a-541f-8d33-b2ba054c50ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438631} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7dbe69a-f051-5886-8cde-e2c1753c8657" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25dae65-1d55-511a-b24e-a8cb350771d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438632} {:answer/id #uuid "41b3f2e3-0dfd-5365-982e-fd4103306c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25dae65-1d55-511a-b24e-a8cb350771d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438632} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8887387-6555-5bd2-8c67-a09c271d9b0f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ebbc31b-8f74-56d3-8d4f-392a8aeeda85" :question/math-academy-id "q-97477" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=-xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af5c066d-bd1b-5916-a240-8a09350060cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438634} {:answer/id #uuid "e033cbd9-4a9c-558a-b84e-515ba29c5965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af5c066d-bd1b-5916-a240-8a09350060cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438634} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f3af7d-b3a9-5b53-9913-e3d5d3053834" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439346} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adc85b15-3131-50c7-b7d4-2e649a9fb136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438636} {:answer/id #uuid "409eb813-3391-5d94-b0e1-4012a919fc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "409eb813-3391-5d94-b0e1-4012a919fc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438637} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc4db1c-cf09-591b-bae6-192098ed008b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439347} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e92256da-0966-5c42-98d2-bb15e2c0f12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438638} {:answer/id #uuid "eea8981c-85f6-57b9-bcd6-78db1679f73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e92256da-0966-5c42-98d2-bb15e2c0f12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438638} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdfcbe44-29db-570c-9bd7-2b09381a9273" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ffe3a0e-6b8b-5f1d-8f45-5dd040381266" :question/math-academy-id "q-97482" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **II.** At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **III.** At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=9xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02991364-b415-5593-9a79-441d1bc3e524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438640} {:answer/id #uuid "8149ff14-001b-5709-8795-c16ffb823815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8149ff14-001b-5709-8795-c16ffb823815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438641} :answer-field/id #uuid "67616cec-be6c-5e26-881c-d38eeb8cbc05" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27aca29d-adac-5da5-979e-bf019c9f7e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438642} {:answer/id #uuid "72677ee7-465d-51d2-8a9e-8bf69e29a86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72677ee7-465d-51d2-8a9e-8bf69e29a86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438643} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d62ff8d-95ed-5dd5-9434-def79d563c0e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d1129e6-cd92-58d0-8181-e4616216f936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438644} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa956fd-de7f-5971-bd20-88593e558254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa956fd-de7f-5971-bd20-88593e558254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438645} :answer-field/id #uuid "572d6ef2-b778-5091-9ae8-150ff55b2bb6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90e895ee-1be8-55c1-ab2d-a3596627f1b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97487" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4x{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **II.** At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/96/96c937cf10d45d8870a4a56e257d184dcf2e4caf11312c05df9f385f9c5ee7a8.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b88eec4-4bca-5a0c-8880-b03758a9562c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438646} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b8d18f-d196-594c-8c33-0a3b063ff4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0b8d18f-d196-594c-8c33-0a3b063ff4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438647} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57f0197-ee41-5615-824b-b099007a4ea7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a982b0-26c1-598f-bb2a-081c92298f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438648} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e21d2c-a5eb-5d88-b6f1-1f1bb6ae21fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09a982b0-26c1-598f-bb2a-081c92298f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438648} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a269a3-6717-5dbd-aa51-fbb9a794d6c2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccb719f3-4ad9-549b-8f43-db9fde533258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438650} {:answer/id #uuid "b9393b52-2c4f-58b9-9b1c-b3c269d74cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9393b52-2c4f-58b9-9b1c-b3c269d74cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e284995b-eb87-5cca-a13d-12a99f42075a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a885d23a-7465-5389-b01f-26298ac26e61" :question/math-academy-id "q-97495" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+2)(y+2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y<-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x+2)(y+2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=-2$ or $y=-2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/7d/7d225d82eee3043380eda76506b28e81aee06d42a232348b91b0d5ceb04f3318.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/bd/bd9eedd3b8d82415e8f3126236f2e3a3b4e326a7072057bbd21406da92a08139.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x>-2$ and $y>-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $x<-2$ and $y>-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x<-2$ and $y<-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4842db83-277f-5ac6-9756-7d71ed57a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438652} {:answer/id #uuid "aff73e0e-7657-51b7-a7f5-1be162f86243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4842db83-277f-5ac6-9756-7d71ed57a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438652} :answer-field/id #uuid "3021006b-6d7f-5efe-b7a1-726f767d922d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32b2ab2-8963-54b9-9a32-d18a9a92c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438654} {:answer/id #uuid "9b16c32e-6930-5573-ab6e-d3adf9fffe07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a32b2ab2-8963-54b9-9a32-d18a9a92c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438654} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9933f56-9026-5d7c-b64d-f7f6c2a23d9e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6c52061-cde6-58fd-9539-45c706276407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438656} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6408d-db98-52dd-9aa1-9d5f8b43eba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6408d-db98-52dd-9aa1-9d5f8b43eba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438657} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab818855-7afa-5726-ad99-5b0158d162ae" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ca0f65d-7276-53ff-adf8-200ee3f85dcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-97534" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **III.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=1$ or $y=1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/07/075264fa042adc64e160a83f03a2b125dd5a9b0927fc3ed318778c0004689ec4.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/57/5716d59a745fc148b504a4db523286d7c7209fe793fcf369a90d220125f7e9fb.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $x<1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $x>1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x>1$ and $y>1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "436bf24b-bdf6-5bd1-bf14-e293249ddd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452431} {:answer/id #uuid "363b7103-4c5b-566a-8bb6-bb6db139809a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "363b7103-4c5b-566a-8bb6-bb6db139809a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452432} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed94cd18-02ea-5163-8871-843e8d20bacd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb395a3-0026-5fe2-8672-0d8a776661b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452433} {:answer/id #uuid "ad49d154-32c7-51a1-9c40-23951e669167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddb395a3-0026-5fe2-8672-0d8a776661b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452433} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f07249-c02b-5278-9c34-c03c810c79e2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453064} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b316a2be-90ba-5f03-af54-19e6e2c49f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452435} {:answer/id #uuid "4beb83e8-81ac-5a00-996b-7891230847a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4beb83e8-81ac-5a00-996b-7891230847a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452436} :answer-field/id #uuid "75b5380c-d718-5a65-a9f5-9759ac621a6b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a9eef89-f664-5ecc-a524-f84c3e33cc1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97871" :question/problem "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n- **III.** $B$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =1$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 1-2+1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (2)^{4}-2(2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 16-8+1 & =0 \\\\ 9 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a42069-f918-5e71-9dd9-2a211c78e924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452437} {:answer/id #uuid "96dc8307-77bd-54e5-ae5b-b3521133ccfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a42069-f918-5e71-9dd9-2a211c78e924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452437} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b11ffe1-abc8-57b0-8f5c-136583ab3529" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453066} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd9b746-af0b-579f-9539-7f4c89d3f3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452439} {:answer/id #uuid "a8139ae7-a53d-5b72-bf22-02323cba44f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8139ae7-a53d-5b72-bf22-02323cba44f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452440} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab27423b-0ac8-5d39-97b6-dba386cf4d90" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3cdd13-53bd-57d7-9494-64f83f7502ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452441} {:answer/id #uuid "e535c4d0-7baa-5a48-acbd-a5afaa7c103d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab3cdd13-53bd-57d7-9494-64f83f7502ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452441} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2becec-7b1e-57b8-92f8-0a9e0aaf26ff" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed9c37d8-c5de-5898-be3f-6a097e562da9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97897" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Bx=0$ has only zero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-0I=B.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-0I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319126a6-cb98-5c29-8c30-bfd20133ec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452443} {:answer/id #uuid "285ae59e-5ddd-5dc9-862d-3bc834ee97db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "285ae59e-5ddd-5dc9-862d-3bc834ee97db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452444} :answer-field/id #uuid "cff45205-5a50-56b9-95a8-9585e7c8f98c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632c7bfe-66b2-5e98-9e93-398f5b32a709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answ