field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fdd6535-d1b6-5f9c-9675-a9cfd3d10476" :question/math-academy-id "q-97946" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(B-4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(B-4I)=4$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B-4I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B-4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=B-4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(B-4I)\\ne 4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=B-4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569444} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570100}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362