6 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcbf4bbb-220b-5800-806a-bc2f67c879d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452459} {:answer/id #uuid "a278fb21-91c2-538a-a6ed-06ef96a955ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a278fb21-91c2-538a-a6ed-06ef96a955ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452460} :answer-field/id #uuid "248362ca-1efd-54c1-b856-0618881c44aa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23a6bd8a-b3a8-5b1e-9289-48d8854d334a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97948" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $C-5I$ is a non-singular matrix.\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(C-5I)=3$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-5I)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9837a523-42e0-533a-a0dd-b42b2ad407d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452461} {:answer/id #uuid "7952bb3e-1500-544d-a00b-8d59f4c87d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7952bb3e-1500-544d-a00b-8d59f4c87d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452462} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f4c341a-3666-5cd8-9f18-b1da0ee0573a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdeda81d-4c04-5009-bc27-1a8a0454cebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452463} {:answer/id #uuid "da0e6b14-88e6-54aa-ad41-1dba2bdc0fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da0e6b14-88e6-54aa-ad41-1dba2bdc0fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452464} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c6c176-24dd-5b33-9a18-8161ff08d029" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453079} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "288f57ed-f71f-52e4-aa4d-9812c1e3a401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452465} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0d6501-f9de-5629-932b-a57f8dc171b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "288f57ed-f71f-52e4-aa4d-9812c1e3a401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452465} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce30fe09-518e-5ee9-b44b-912d5a059e61" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1c970e5-75bc-5ce7-946f-a4ae8b32b111" :question/math-academy-id "q-97960" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $C-I$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(C-I)\\ne 2$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(C-I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-I)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487555} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488316} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569444} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570047}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "775c219f-a459-55cb-a155-865e3fee7ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452467} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffb7501-a137-566a-82b1-7897016fb94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ffb7501-a137-566a-82b1-7897016fb94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452468} :answer-field/id #uuid "365204e9-984b-5368-9d39-59ebec92b9e6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fe10ad-b6fd-51a0-ae35-69c2a8fbad6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452469} {:answer/id #uuid "082ed9ac-ae1b-5302-8e14-5b74ae93b90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25fe10ad-b6fd-51a0-ae35-69c2a8fbad6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452469} :answer-field/id #uuid "470374fb-21bf-5e7b-9721-41f5a726ab31" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b81eebb0-7957-5576-828c-99384c50a06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452471} {:answer/id #uuid "b516c0cf-2ba1-5ba9-8852-4382e9ac3ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b81eebb0-7957-5576-828c-99384c50a06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452471} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8bd4bfb-634d-5ae5-aa62-0dafe1569a4e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7aa7ebc3-80d5-541f-813e-64c8db0cc171" :question/math-academy-id "q-97962" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}$\n- **II.** $A-3I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A-3I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A-3I) \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is non-singular, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2cedf5f-ea78-598b-8fea-86eb4930a16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452473} {:answer/id #uuid "ac501363-d562-542c-8971-fa619b64fb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac501363-d562-542c-8971-fa619b64fb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452474} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed2e22e-171c-554d-a637-c726d3aaa309" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "941dacde-a17c-5765-910f-5bc4839a1386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452475} {:answer/id #uuid "5f487188-0cec-5f38-9e34-e190f507bb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f487188-0cec-5f38-9e34-e190f507bb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452476} :answer-field/id #uuid "611e09b5-b47a-5519-9300-6bc0ece8a2f0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0044e830-172d-574e-b097-e1ebe25e1adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452477} {:answer/id #uuid "4200c14b-bbe6-5725-adaa-903da877df9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0044e830-172d-574e-b097-e1ebe25e1adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452477} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef6a8d9-228c-5e0b-8e7a-462c8a409290" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "651bcec7-c2ce-52d1-b1e2-d8f7e6384008" :question/math-academy-id "q-97969" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-3I)=4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$\n- **II.** $A-3I$ is invertible\n- **III.** $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(A-3I) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is invertible, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487555} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488346} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569444} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570071}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d33d9c39-fb68-5072-8e1d-d5100f882d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452479} {:answer/id #uuid "31f89fd5-7b10-551c-bc20-cccf8c2d412f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d33d9c39-fb68-5072-8e1d-d5100f882d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452479} :answer-field/id #uuid "51a700a6-056d-5fbf-9c15-802b0bdd40bd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f33abab6-7da2-5b60-9d2f-3f60b47ce8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452481} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9de0a6-d221-54ed-ad32-0d48a983af48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d9de0a6-d221-54ed-ad32-0d48a983af48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452482} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec61757-aa1d-5552-94ae-87ef06754a16" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9364d49b-0c70-5f53-9afc-58a6465fdfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452483} {:answer/id #uuid "bb43d0d6-435a-59ca-b0d6-dd54c6d2f55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9364d49b-0c70-5f53-9afc-58a6465fdfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452483} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d70d8de-2041-5147-b2df-9b23496268ca" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb578155-2783-5751-8e62-1deb89778f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-97970" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(B-I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(B-I)={R}^{3}$\n- **II.** $B-I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =1$ is not an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(B-I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(B-I) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(B-I)\\ne 3,$ according to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(B-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B-I$ is singular, then $(B-I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2408efc0-effa-5d1a-95bf-4e5888eb4e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452485} {:answer/id #uuid "478fc76b-1ffb-5eba-9444-fb9a5daab721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2408efc0-effa-5d1a-95bf-4e5888eb4e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452485} :answer-field/id #uuid "298cd9f6-0f4b-5d91-b1e2-380da3c049cf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453090} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890097e8-1b87-5c8b-80f8-73adc10117b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452487} {:answer/id #uuid "194e8931-2c02-5f42-b19b-625e045d7857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "890097e8-1b87-5c8b-80f8-73adc10117b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452487} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a5a7b14-0732-5180-838e-5db043472ec8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88db7284-406b-5896-82c0-024eb4192579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452489} {:answer/id #uuid "35417b18-69fa-55aa-bd77-4e154b50f8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35417b18-69fa-55aa-bd77-4e154b50f8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452490} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d73097d-9167-5ae9-ac40-5d49927706d1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "89915cc7-c25f-5711-a62f-10d7e3f1b8b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97972" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(C-2I)={R}^{2}$\n- **II.** $C-2I$ is non-singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(C-2I) & =2-\\text{nullity}(C-2I) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C-2I$ is singular, then $(C-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4ccb0fb-57b3-54cc-9a0e-7b3b2ec086c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452491} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0736bf-0992-5540-92e4-4db3dc860d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4ccb0fb-57b3-54cc-9a0e-7b3b2ec086c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a2ad23-7a6f-5d92-beff-9522a7a1c6e4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453093} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4c9d99e-8bcf-530d-a35b-845c74343724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452493} {:answer/id #uuid "b94d0759-6f74-5338-954d-fc2d4ed8aa4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b94d0759-6f74-5338-954d-fc2d4ed8aa4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452494} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabb1107-ac4d-5f76-9ad8-0708cf4bdbd2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee5ed75-acac-56ef-98ed-57d5822de8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452495} {:answer/id #uuid "537989db-6a16-5fcc-aa37-3d4aa93c947c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "537989db-6a16-5fcc-aa37-3d4aa93c947c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452496} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f83e84-79e0-5f94-a3f0-7b675d59f092" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68bc418d-6421-5050-b933-2a80a591867e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97975" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(C+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(C+3I)={0}$\n- **II.** $C+3I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C+3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(C+3I) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C+3I)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C+3I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C+3I$ is singular, then $(C+3I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487555} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488290}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655 :topic/id #uuid "b2c456e2-ef0a-5a75-95da-68bb5f61da4a" :topic/math-academy-id 3356 :topic/title "Simple, Closed, and Oriented Curves"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b56df147-449a-5ae1-aa90-5d246df31e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469890} {:answer