nswer-field/id #uuid "a3a892cb-020f-5b99-a1b4-9020867438f8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470559} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cf05806-10fd-5532-9e8e-c9e94658c5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469896} {:answer/id #uuid "efea8fd8-91cd-54d7-b040-e4a3c89c7207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cf05806-10fd-5532-9e8e-c9e94658c5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469896} :answer-field/id #uuid "07877319-c0c7-581a-ba16-3bf298a39f19" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0e004a8-576b-5fb6-94f2-3e66cfd74da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-98338" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1b6a9fa72404e765796d75fc7ba00a391317ef2670e2f65f7c43db32207e8d2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not closed.\n\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve III is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is not simple.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741 :topic/id #uuid "10441679-94f4-5ae1-8bff-8e5945144d9c" :topic/math-academy-id 2532 :topic/title "Phase Lines of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641505e3-4fec-567c-9c83-85531b7e7c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469898} {:answer/id #uuid "8c339b98-1358-5e7d-8176-5121ba8d7389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "641505e3-4fec-567c-9c83-85531b7e7c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469898} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8358553-9442-58c8-8202-498365ddb0e4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "522e362c-1cf2-5e5e-bf1e-ef974c5457f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469900} {:answer/id #uuid "daaa37b4-08b7-53d2-adb1-dcb759580aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "522e362c-1cf2-5e5e-bf1e-ef974c5457f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469900} :answer-field/id #uuid "18be5b77-e262-5137-851e-aadfcd89dc06" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c57acda6-1c6a-5883-9027-7b21a18b0906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469902} {:answer/id #uuid "0a5ae1cb-d891-5e47-9927-87731b1815d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a5ae1cb-d891-5e47-9927-87731b1815d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469903} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd41de6-cafd-59b8-852b-7d4b0662b46c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cdb362d-f774-5da6-bc15-c04006a3144a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98632" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca296c3a8a2989fd5e5ca647ad4f7d0f4890aa3caf8d9e0a1fce98498d2d7d64.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741 :topic/id #uuid "10441679-94f4-5ae1-8bff-8e5945144d9c" :topic/math-academy-id 2532 :topic/title "Phase Lines of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d8db14d-ee62-5f27-9e1d-94fabe34d298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469904} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fc96b7-4a76-5f62-a63c-72f8b35a31dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3fc96b7-4a76-5f62-a63c-72f8b35a31dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469905} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c7181d-3159-5eb8-8297-7334eac26868" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce722e7d-8ced-56bb-8b95-f9228089b504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469906} {:answer/id #uuid "51b5c6a0-f8a9-5df5-96b9-6b8e0cb79e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce722e7d-8ced-56bb-8b95-f9228089b504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469906} :answer-field/id #uuid "cedc6c80-dac3-5be1-b567-60fe9aa755cd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "886dc1a9-31f9-5e08-a537-41e9c2a3e5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469908} {:answer/id #uuid "ba36d9ea-19f6-5bd9-8316-4a6de407a017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "886dc1a9-31f9-5e08-a537-41e9c2a3e5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469908} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fbef377-40b9-5ebf-8afc-8f580c32d99f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98ee9b06-5212-535a-b3fd-5d1e48bb48f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-98636" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y({y}^{2}-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(4-{y}^{2})$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-4$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=y({y}^{2}-4).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=y(y+2)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(4-{y}^{2}).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(4-{y}^{2}),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)={y}^{2}-4,$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=\\pm 2$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741 :topic/id #uuid "10441679-94f4-5ae1-8bff-8e5945144d9c" :topic/math-academy-id 2532 :topic/title "Phase Lines of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec7790f1-e74c-577a-9fac-f4ad0834e7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469916} {:answer/id #uuid "544703b8-aee8-5172-a235-fe2dd9d223f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec7790f1-e74c-577a-9fac-f4ad0834e7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469916} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d06c494-d9ab-58f8-8aa4-790cf3c7e1f5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8fc17b6-9029-54fe-8151-860ee2a28ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469918} {:answer/id #uuid "f60f0f4a-cd20-50b1-86bc-47b454ff1491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60f0f4a-cd20-50b1-86bc-47b454ff1491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469919} :answer-field/id #uuid "01975edc-764b-5829-a3ff-c04974e897a0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc85124-4b3f-5452-8d24-ba7bad684c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469920} {:answer/id #uuid "f0710156-43ac-518c-bd75-c5d772369c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fc85124-4b3f-5452-8d24-ba7bad684c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469920} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21e23dc-b213-5dcc-ab9b-58c4fbcd44f2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77dc20d1-343d-5ade-92ad-7f182610538c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98643" :question/problem "![](images/96/96740fdcc0dc6aa22fa097ba714669fae4ef79543aafad72b3fc3db7722f83d1.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2y$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-y(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (0,2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-y(y-2)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-y$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,0),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=y(y+2),$ and the equilibrium solution are $y=-2,0.$ \n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741 :topic/id #uuid "10441679-94f4-5ae1-8bff-8e5945144d9c" :topic/math-academy-id 2532 :topic/title "Phase Lines of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df839928-3f6f-55e0-8ce3-ec178f216622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469922} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6dbeeb-7472-57c7-bccd-8dabac257990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df839928-3f6f-55e0-8ce3-ec178f216622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469922} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee4327d-dc7a-5d44-8201-3e5c06fed984" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470573} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1a270f2-6e76-58ad-b29f-a44eb2c0ae8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469924} {:answer/id #uuid "625f9e50-ee73-5f92-a569-3766007bff23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1a270f2-6e76-58ad-b29f-a44eb2c0ae8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469924} :answer-field/id #uuid "44758402-cc1c-5952-aa33-e66b0505929c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470574} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fab95f-3e9a-5af1-a273-39130055d0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469926} {:answer/id #uuid "4d49f84f-a402-5b92-826f-647f032b17bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d49f84f-a402-5b92-826f-647f032b17bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469927} :answer-field/id #uuid "88057ab1-b1cf-5762-bfd3-e9cb8a2c8d02" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23af5cf8-2a07-56b8-9952-fd82e54a4b1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98646" :question/problem "![](images/54/54cfcbaa5ce20c1881f35e83bb0ec28fa1399cc5100ee745941ff7bb52ac7705.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+2)(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y+2)(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y+2)(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1af47ea-6253-5d58-9b94-31e52de27106" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Simply-Connected Regions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050189 :topic/id #uuid "34f05255-901a-5db2-9431-e0175ef37691" :topic/math-academy-id 3357 :topic/title "Connected and Simply-Connected Regions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fffacc9e-beec-5b87-a6ad-4054725f30a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469934} {:answer/id #uuid "5a491a09-f7b0-54c0-8c16-31aefd05fbc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a491a09-f7b0-54c0-8c16-31aefd05fbc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469935} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc494cca-9c27-5636-819c-d7ba48ac2365" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c593b10-83f5-57ab-b0fa-86ecb233c7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469936} {:answer/id #uuid "4172b6dc-4962-502e-91c0-4be1831ffab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4172b6dc-4962-502e-91c0-4be1831ffab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469937} :answer-field/id #uuid "e392a014-4c1c-5154-b560-d0b81245c899" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "771cd02f-fda7-5ee6-af63-ec59da5fabdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469938} {:answer/id #uuid "765b46eb-b850-5e76-81ad-42e64146ea0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "765b46eb-b850-5e76-81ad-42e64146ea0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469939} :answer-field/id #uuid "648a2bf6-5faa-57ac-a014-76cffeaf60dc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470581} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b01d9637-3916-5456-83d5-9644992bb85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469940} {:answer/id #uuid "950b233d-c663-5478-b3de-db1d19d6b842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b01d9637-3916-5456-83d5-9644992bb85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469940} :answer-field/id #uuid "84cbe606-0544-50d5-b199-a029d91a2906" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0317a51a-f6d5-582a-aa69-b3f89cc6775d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98690" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0db1568fab302ddbd9f16dbdf9c276eeb62cbac37346b077c1639fe68c2396ac.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131 :topic/id #uuid "81e52842-e868-5110-93ce-65b6024b5770" :topic/math-academy-id 2533 :topic/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dffa19b1-021e-5bc9-bf4b-107c83df34f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469942} {:answer/id #uuid "95e69d35-7b77-5aaf-b2dc-bcb739f4cb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95e69d35-7b77-5aaf-b2dc-bcb739f4cb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469943} :answer-field/id #uuid "013f2b96-c94d-5f99-8cba-0c4626be8053" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470583} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b6d1b8e-ba76-5d4b-be1f-a44787b64217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469944} {:answer/id #uuid "e65a15db-5abf-5997-a11d-029ad1f6f1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b6d1b8e-ba76-5d4b-be1f-a44787b64217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469944} :answer-field/id #uuid "32941a7c-78b7-5b28-b4e6-d72b784332e9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470584} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b753abe-4f14-5521-b106-57d1127e8877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469946} {:answer/id #uuid "0a90ca89-05d8-5fca-9858-83c9597d5a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a90ca89-05d8-5fca-9858-83c9597d5a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469947} :answer-field/id #uuid "025e9360-3cd8-5d39-bf8a-f1efb90e9011" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd0fe6f6-6555-578d-880a-04a4380b3213" :question/math-academy-id "q-98700" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c4ef140c85eb91bd01688cc2a447f13a21c2a51964c6d101887d15045cee95d.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=-2$ is a sink\n\nII. $y=-2$ is a source\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a node\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-2.$This means that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=2.$ This means that $y=2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131 :topic/id #uuid "81e52842-e868-5110-93ce-65b6024b5770" :topic/math-academy-id 2533 :topic/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ed1e406-3c33-526a-928d-45d7b29222b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469948} {:answer/id #uuid "f65b9d2c-9c73-59e8-b6d4-54161a0564a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f65b9d2c-9c73-59e8-b6d4-54161a0564a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469949} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8efa5ed-ff7f-5cd9-8458-782f0d2278ba" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470586} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa2358d-b6cf-5fbf-ad23-fba7b6dbd93c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469950} {:answer/id #uuid "c0776d1b-64d3-5f59-b68a-b0e47d0f29dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0aa2358d-b6cf-5fbf-ad23-fba7b6dbd93c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469950} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bf70f99-2936-53b2-b761-c32f09eebf00" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470587} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a539960-8b3f-5e68-838a-627520c241ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469952} {:answer/id #uuid "770692ad-d884-51bb-9502-e7c9271205d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "770692ad-d884-51bb-9502-e7c9271205d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469953} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eecb54f-ce8d-5d82-ab1e-8786be1614cf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3fbe51b-8dce-56ec-a759-a07e5a4e34b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98701" :question/problem "![](images/74/74790c4dbcb2d9cebee802dd155b5fa0a94e442f9bbc59c6277eb0d6fca3d60a.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=-5$ is a node\n- $y=-5$ is a sink\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=-2.$ This means that $y=-2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-5$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-5.$ This means that $y=-5$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131 :topic/id #uuid "81e52842-e868-5110-93ce-65b6024b5770" :topic/math-academy-id 2533 :topic/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2da37e5-630c-5f38-90a0-11ab54236a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469954} {:answer/id #uuid "c125c7f2-c5dc-5e7a-85ca-7b9394119daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c125c7f2-c5dc-5e7a-85ca-7b9394119daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469955} :answer-field/id #uuid "39d9d5fe-2136-5657-8b1d-4946df15ce64" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd1ddc6-d8e0-5d38-90dd-c7482b68dddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469956} {:answer/id #uuid "b06ddfeb-7163-5e42-9ebd-f68ec2f6041e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd1ddc6-d8e0-5d38-90dd-c7482b68dddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469956} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b2d89ac-d63f-58e1-bb03-4e1dcdebb45a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470590} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5a59d50-5e8f-5016-a985-1b5875409a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469958} {:answer/id #uuid "5d661de3-3374-58bb-ab93-474b0db44f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5a59d50-5e8f-5016-a985-1b5875409a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469958} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1e04ee-6938-5a40-add2-de859fe32ae9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf8ec5e0-4855-5481-a220-299eb2c89a02" :question/math-academy-id "q-98702" :question/problem "![](images/68/68eb28d31145fe38d481cb2273bc35edc80bfccf1b80b2e9a75b4136eab45629.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source\n- $y=4$ is a node\n- $y=1$ is a sink\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=4$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=4.$ This means that $y=4$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=1$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side oft $y=1.$This means that $y=1$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131 :topic/id #uuid "81e52842-e868-5110-93ce-65b6024b5770" :topic/math-academy-id 2533 :topic/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d01b33f2-8671-5b0d-8a00-a92c63ec823d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469960} {:answer/id #uuid "0db0ab70-5d9b-5ca1-96a8-1e2b6c6570ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0db0ab70-5d9b-5ca1-96a8-1e2b6c6570ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469961} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db18589-06db-5ac2-bf04-cfba198ae3db" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470592} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cae1d0b-65c4-5a6a-855e-7a286a4599be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469962} {:answer/id #uuid "3af7659c-fd25-5191-bb88-4984d69dd78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3af7659c-fd25-5191-bb88-4984d69dd78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text}