wer/id #uuid "92445541-35c4-5fec-8155-d89e8ad738bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470012} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5f7120-ecc0-57f5-83c8-5c9716762bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd5f7120-ecc0-57f5-83c8-5c9716762bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470013} :answer-field/id #uuid "03813ec3-f377-5abb-a21d-dde851c389d8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c64aee5-b8e4-5802-9d88-5d5ccb488483" :question/math-academy-id "q-99839" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-5t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4t)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )}{6})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72eb4719-e98f-5cb9-a9e4-ea5ac615be39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470014} {:answer/id #uuid "b831ddfd-2e75-57a3-803c-fce7fe0f20f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b831ddfd-2e75-57a3-803c-fce7fe0f20f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470015} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a5031a-4e64-5a88-9ff5-3c50b425c755" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470619} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61342765-a723-57af-9b7c-1e66b2684508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470016} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd08386-f78d-5999-af5f-11dcacc7153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fd08386-f78d-5999-af5f-11dcacc7153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470017} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbf0f0e-0c01-528a-9359-099c9f7fa8e3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfceee01-94dd-5b9c-9d9e-e30c9256d930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470018} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c8d57c-bf1b-5f00-8da9-25440a3756bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8c8d57c-bf1b-5f00-8da9-25440a3756bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470019} :answer-field/id #uuid "4425320b-ac0f-5887-8acf-749452911dc2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8889f645-9334-518f-83dc-7064dd4f1d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99848" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of continuous functions over $[-2,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot (t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{2}^{5}-(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "784a2850-d785-530f-aa35-86727745985b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470020} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2d9506-b045-5926-9142-18cc1607cd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b2d9506-b045-5926-9142-18cc1607cd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470021} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b6c08d9-5d1c-502e-b929-fe555d39f868" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470622} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab30298-ee35-5c65-b0b5-bb5c7c154973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470022} {:answer/id #uuid "19296e56-4751-5c98-a40c-58a9373c78a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19296e56-4751-5c98-a40c-58a9373c78a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470023} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d6fd86c-420c-5568-946d-e6e0fc6b7efa" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470623} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195e9cc4-8791-5538-b2cb-896116884dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470024} {:answer/id #uuid "4686a6d2-44af-5fd2-8610-552030f9a4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4686a6d2-44af-5fd2-8610-552030f9a4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470025} :answer-field/id #uuid "c24d6f99-befe-551b-a5a7-e296425c2c84" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "021a928d-5bbc-5ce6-b2a2-f571107e0c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-99875" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(1)+3(-1)(3)+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =1-9+8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(3)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(-1)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2990f090-ca0e-52b0-80e4-7aed1d344b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470026} {:answer/id #uuid "999c54fc-b4e2-5af7-9647-40c00e72e1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "999c54fc-b4e2-5af7-9647-40c00e72e1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470027} :answer-field/id #uuid "24157043-12b2-5085-9a13-e837fc9a370f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470625} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3294878-5b62-5564-9835-061f195a5554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470028} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0f8555-b5a3-51e7-84c7-f43468499842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3294878-5b62-5564-9835-061f195a5554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470028} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec699f6-533f-5681-aa5b-02ddec0c152d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470626} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c85da57c-2560-53b5-8df7-05ec51158ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470030} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2cce26-1ade-538c-9f50-75653621411b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e2cce26-1ade-538c-9f50-75653621411b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470031} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5d99a9-325e-5b8c-8282-62ae5a43df7b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a41208f-2a14-503d-a1ad-b859e476f0c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-99878" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =2(1)(5)+(1)(-2)+(2)(-4) \\\\  & =10-2-8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(1)(0)+(1)(2)+(2)(-1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(5)(0)+(-2)(2)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =0-4+4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b37083a7-aaaa-5a2a-973a-5c9ff944dfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470032} {:answer/id #uuid "aade6615-cdec-5bb3-9ebc-b52ba6498acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aade6615-cdec-5bb3-9ebc-b52ba6498acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470033} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee6465c-b492-5dcd-9a5b-72f46a3a5104" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470628} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dcfb967-51e5-5257-b6ea-f6b21931da14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470034} {:answer/id #uuid "d6927137-4c46-5fd0-a3de-bc83e11cc13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dcfb967-51e5-5257-b6ea-f6b21931da14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470034} :answer-field/id #uuid "f55811ff-e66f-5a0e-812a-3b75349cc59e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec93396-6ade-5b54-b2dc-4c3f0308a1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470036} {:answer/id #uuid "42e1db1a-4eb1-5170-b19b-b7d378bf5855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ec93396-6ade-5b54-b2dc-4c3f0308a1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470036} :answer-field/id #uuid "8547b10e-8dd7-5314-9138-e70dea301350" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d313e454-e9a2-5532-b1b1-37c4d7cbbe80" :question/math-academy-id "q-99951" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{2}(t)=t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}(t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-2-(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"} :activity/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517479} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518233}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1edb45bd-5686-5bd3-a5e8-39434b7936e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451305} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdaca35-4a5c-5775-b375-7f5a62df0513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fdaca35-4a5c-5775-b375-7f5a62df0513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451306} :answer-field/id #uuid "fda9bab3-48aa-590a-8fbb-e6278bd3979b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452500} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f942fb-0b51-5721-a798-3ef7b79f314c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451307} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b2bb2e-ef53-5665-874e-acf997870797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27f942fb-0b51-5721-a798-3ef7b79f314c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451307} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ae7022-ac6d-50ac-949c-c361f871d0d9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452501} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a867c05-749f-52ca-8d48-1539d08a5b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451309} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0f0e57-f4cb-5e3a-b938-abd9cd17f104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb0f0e57-f4cb-5e3a-b938-abd9cd17f104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451310} :answer-field/id #uuid "70ed7379-1768-5e75-a724-8e30b56031fa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c08bbf4-33fd-53d2-9498-4e914b813e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109239" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+I$ and $A-2I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n- **II.** $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n- **III.** $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+1I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }v,\n$$\n\n and $v\\ne 0,$ \nthe vector $u$ is **not**  an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e150edfa-4d44-5ab4-b043-ba48e414155f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468876} {:answer/id #uuid "4e202e38-3b7c-56bb-b632-db0087893be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e150edfa-4d44-5ab4-b043-ba48e414155f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468876} :answer-field/id #uuid "16b1f541-6829-5760-b5cd-2bf997f751ec" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daff9470-1403-525c-a778-8cd8a3abbaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468878} {:answer/id #uuid "411c8d9b-b9e8-5e53-9add-722476432317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "411c8d9b-b9e8-5e53-9add-722476432317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468879} :answer-field/id #uuid "d99a7be7-c36c-521b-a83f-222874be7a98" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ae47d9b-d794-5484-a1b3-59518fd10b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468880} {:answer/id #uuid "52e13f5f-43af-5535-afd2-29e2add95b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ae47d9b-d794-5484-a1b3-59518fd10b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468880} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d70e912-f00f-557a-98ae-ac4eb7d47693" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92a6911e-532d-5f89-96e6-761d0ceb55f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109487" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aedcb32b-f84c-5aa2-b3a4-f7e83746b836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468882} {:answer/id #uuid "aed73398-59c2-51b4-9371-f0f65e98014f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aedcb32b-f84c-5aa2-b3a4-f7e83746b836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468882} :answer-field/id #uuid "9958dde5-b255-5d95-ae9b-baca80f743d5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3915fd0e-f480-5ebd-983a-af8001f5e607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468884} {:answer/id #uuid "23790615-5ac5-5c45-97e2-001e01eff0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3915fd0e-f480-5ebd-983a-af8001f5e607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468884} :answer-field/id #uuid "03f1a276-3cb9-581b-9099-1990fdc1c513" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a54f6205-db26-5c00-8932-ebcca124f521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468886} {:answer/id #uuid "40b5715b-352e-56b1-a124-d548af347d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a54f6205-db26-5c00-8932-ebcca124f521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468886} :answer-field/id #uuid "1659548f-da18-5fc1-9233-0906388e6725" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c9089c-246d-51fb-b493-4538acc22376" :question/math-academy-id "q-109574" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200 :topic/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7d2435-e6d8-50bc-9006-6cd7c923b1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468888} {:answer/id #uuid "350a6cf1-0f8b-5dac-8762-1b097f5c4719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "350a6cf1-0f8b-5dac-8762-1b097f5c4719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468889} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fe84bd-8474-5fcd-a77c-7a3c567193e5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f93c3f6-6f23-5141-af32-ead12f4b0584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468890} {:answer/id #uuid "cae9d183-09d0-59e5-81fc-1eacd146d5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f93c3f6-6f23-5141-af32-ead12f4b0584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468890} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0571b0-7e44-5665-b133-86da9da8ed90" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8e58602-dd41-5ce2-b415-2c830458e030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468892} {:answer/id #uuid "db5d008d-ddfc-52dd-9d86-955672919e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8e58602-dd41-5ce2-b415-2c830458e030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468892} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bd2c2e-b550-5aa0-9c59-4e4d8badbbaf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b12af04-ad27-575e-b0d1-aaf05c9b69e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109774" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ for every matrix $A?$\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(A)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Null}(A)$\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ is the left null space of $A.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${A}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$, that is, each column of $A$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200 :topic/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b99cf61-4188-5177-9243-e43b4bd470a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468894} {:answer/id #uuid "52f8e72f-d7a5-57c3-963c-58d1ae177c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b99cf61-4188-5177-9243-e43b4bd470a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468894} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d934aee-1837-5fd6-b7e9-31803fca2c1a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61cd6959-f3ae-5281-a702-a6f1fbb4b347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468896} {:answer/id #uuid "c766b5e4-f8e9-5807-a6a9-6eea349022f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61cd6959-f3ae-5281-a702-a6f1fbb4b347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468896} :answer-field/id #uuid "e560755c-de37-53f3-abf3-cf0ebd912b36" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c64afc9-c9f8-5334-bf63-252b9c9011c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468898} {:answer/id #uuid "540b90b9-15b3-5bc0-9d0c-5195e70e08e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "540b90b9-15b3-5bc0-9d0c-5195e70e08e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468899} :answer-field/id #uuid "feb95007-7157-56ac-b69d-8e8b3627d71d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90aa20cc-7bd4-5b9c-96fe-e3f55743e36c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109781" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ for every matrix $B?$\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(B)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(B)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ is the row space of $B.$ This consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $B.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $B,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $B.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $B,$ we actually multiply each row of $B$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Null}(B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200 :topic/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a086316-7979-5489-80d1-53acc4d24abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468900} {:answer/id #uuid "b99411da-dd65-540c-baac-33222e1d3119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b99411da-dd65-540c-baac-33222e1d3119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468901} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbddedc3-e46b-5894-a531-254e9b2af1f7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03dbe6ba-2210-5d10-b9dc-7784b2974743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468902} {:answer/id #uuid "175690fb-4a79-5f7a-9ebf-d563a8b6c20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03dbe6ba-2210-5d10-b9dc-7784b2974743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468902} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e51fd03-ef62-5d86-b235-8ff005960dca" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3b55218-0017-54d5-844f-7cadc727ad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468904} {:answer/id #uuid "659815a3-3997-5a2d-8572-21b4da63ec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b55218-0017-54d5-844f-7cadc727ad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468904} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fd6a5f6-5f26-5807-8c29-f09159124c60" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c3520bf-5736-51de-a6b7-f6ed37ab8f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109852" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(B)$ for every matrix $B?$\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(B)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(B)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $B$, that is, the rows of ${B}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${B}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${B}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${B}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${B}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(B)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Null}({B}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722 :topic/id #uuid "0e116f4e-27ca-5606-81f6-7aa01e4cd9b8" :topic/math-academy-id 3220 :topic/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f3fb44a-5490-5c19-8dee-85b2abd2004f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468906} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccb2efc-9aa5-581b-9dc6-3bccac908663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f3fb44a-5490-5c19-8dee-85b2abd2004f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468906} :answer-field/id #uuid "416ef8e1-f182-5bc0-84b4-7491d68b70e9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470065} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b29f257d-ce75-5d81-9686-9ed494387614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468908} {:answer/id #uuid "e8823ee4-d8ab-5aa2-a2f8-a5040cb6e2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b29f257d-ce75-5d81-9686-9ed494387614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468908} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb2b31b-82e4-563c-9c5a-d93987854026" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470066} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ffdd4bd-e260-5e69-8280-f2f66fdbf870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468910} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ce93f0-7bb1-5161-8005-5dfa196e656f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7ce93f0-7bb1-5161-8005-5dfa196e656f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468911} :answer-field/id #uuid "de5792cc-f766-54d2-a12e-8e918370da4a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81af22bb-892f-5bfc-b62b-3a9ae21c3aa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-116029" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b25629c102be82d96b7ca94d5ab5ce499d327837ed53c6b4adb69ee214db81d.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/1b/1bf59ce83d7a82899587095217c080a5942f28a9c36c6971ec07ae5e8988e7fc.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$ and negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\pm \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to \\pm 1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$ and negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722 :topic/id #uuid "0e116f4e-27ca-5606-81f6-7aa01e4cd9b8" :topic/math-academy-id 3220 :topic/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c69f5100-ae5e-5d0b-a4e2-2b4e296f1692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468912} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9a4e18-e2aa-5215-8483-ad1503727431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d9a4e18-e2aa-5215-8483-ad1503727431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468913} :answer-field/id #uuid "a407c0f1-4ac9-5951-acec-5ce5128ad46d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67c39275-0d40-5339-aeb1-f2563bb4e87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468914} {:answer/id #uuid "4b05d876-a276-5668-a2de-f0cbeb627d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c39275-0d40-5339-aeb1-f2563bb4e87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468914} :answer-field/id #uuid "80ec1d2c-9883-500d-a634-079999b4e224" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6713e510-0e8d-5620-ac28-f28b687c4f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468916} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8c48b0-f144-5f90-912f-56f40874ae90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6713e510-0e8d-5620-ac28-f28b687c4f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468916} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c04d0d-05ca-5591-b260-dd6ea338584a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1bc4fffb-9fca-5d90-ae58-a105216385f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120187" :question/problem "![](images/da/da8d6b73685dbd2db82ad6bdaa528f316fb7819ba3f99f226f0ad9127d3a5609.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y+1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/9b/9b6c8c113f59cc006f26e336b85abde6337a3af2a9f5d6dccc001eee636991ac.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\pm \\infty ,$ and $|{y}^{′}|\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{y+1}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445 :topic/id #uuid "a01c36d3-5643-5685-845e-29c37e89238f" :topic/math-academy-id 1733 :topic/title "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4b2e2b5-5692-5b4a-804f-7518ab214d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437872} {:answer/id #uuid "3acb46da-0035-5f87-8672-6682f18cf559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3acb46da-0035-5f87-8672-6682f18cf559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437873} :answer-field/id #uuid "477f40a4-66af-5870-95c1-78ba941c5598" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438965} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7886ec-2b7c-5184-ba5d-a9cfd3f447ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437874} {:answer/id #uuid "1b86766c-211b-5526-8849-1753bc7e2812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c7886ec-2b7c-5184-ba5d-a9cfd3f447ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437874} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f4b7f4-de99-5ebc-a3fb-5f5fcbe4139b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438966} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d52184b-23ff-5d3d-be0f-8e48b9e54a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437876} {:answer/id #uuid "545708f8-29e7-5393-a590-6497da645a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d52184b-23ff-5d3d-be0f-8e48b9e54a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437876} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b08613c-08bd-5a65-b4c6-d5cae5341709" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8de2435-5f56-5017-bf79-0b535c719854" :question/math-academy-id "q-131718" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y+z &=2 \\\\ -2x+7y+2z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system has no solution\n- **III.** The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we can find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130 :topic/id #uuid "2f232786-4262-5d64-9c58-1578e8c629d2" :topic/math-academy-id 2129 :topic/title "QR Factorization"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd89ee68-349c-5c55-bfb4-0640100d85d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451317} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0d0530-fb2a-55ee-8c99-4b946a8d080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f0d0530-fb2a-55ee-8c99-4b946a8d080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451318} :answer-field/id #uuid "32ce223a-7bf9-5b24-9d75-5a1ee1be8cee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452506} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80aff2bb-c529-532f-bd61-5a4a757274f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451319} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8b817d-950d-56d2-a00f-a66cc027dd62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80aff2bb-c529-532f-bd61-5a4a757274f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451319} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9e0400-ff35-582c-afa0-5b642cb65d38" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452507} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20a8fdb-8267-5e02-9526-345d5877f1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451321} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bdf3d-57db-5827-9f8f-4a457f333e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bdf3d-57db-5827-9f8f-4a457f333e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451322} :answer-field/id #uuid "46443e21-8812-5903-b11e-26709577b9c0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ea55d3c-3433-59ef-97d4-45fcd98ab0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-140896" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $5\\times 4$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The columns of $Q$ are orthogonal\n\n- **II.** $Q$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix\n\n- **III.** $R$ is a  square  matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are orthogonal.\n\n- Statement II is false. $Q$ should be a $5\\times 4$  matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be a $4\\times 4$ upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130 :topic/id #uuid "2f232786-4262-5d64-9c58-1578e8c629d2" :topic/math-academy-id 2129 :topic/title "QR Factorization"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef9dba6-3f12-5da9-b1c0-57877064fcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451323} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccbf279-9ed0-556d-9395-eaabb82722b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ef9dba6-3f12-5da9-b1c0-57877064fcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451323} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e589c4-8feb-50f2-94e5-91dc5bf78939" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47423d03-c26d-53a6-97f0-da0ec40e24ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451325} {:answer/id #uuid "59929f29-b3e2-5f68-ad2f-45acb0eee989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59929f29-b3e2-5f68-ad2f-45acb0eee989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451326} :answer-field/id #uuid "947adca6-648c-56d1-8cd2-dda98433a243" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d589cba1-1f3b-5854-9859-9dd1c62ec2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451327} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c8f22c-d237-5016-ab0c-213ce6e6e57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d589cba1-1f3b-5854-9859-9dd1c62ec2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451327} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee67563d-5792-5feb-9fa1-bed677e18320" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1442b18-9e38-5f57-94c8-12000a66d6da" :question/math-academy-id "q-140897" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Q$ is an $n\\times m$  matrix\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)$ is spanned by the columns of $Q$\n- **III.** $R$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ they span $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ must be an $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3443de64-0676-5770-92c8-866233b3cf8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451329} {:answer/id #uuid "901fe7d0-00db-5d2b-bbbe-46afcbf47bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3443de64-0676-5770-92c8-866233b3cf8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451329} :answer-field/id #uuid "92a67864-fc33-5ecf-8c84-7b60097bcbae" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452512} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86a924bc-04c3-5242-9b40-d555e76f1a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451331} {:answer/id #uuid "7007492f-f2e7-51ca-a49a-711d1d61f5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86a924bc-04c3-5242-9b40-d555e76f1a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451331} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a1eb8ec-220b-56c6-9fb0-7009727726eb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c634fe05-9622-5bb0-8a7a-383e16e613d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451333} {:answer/id #uuid "298354bf-6e73-5d58-bebe-1e6824b6781d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "298354bf-6e73-5d58-bebe-1e6824b6781d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451334} :answer-field/id #uuid "51cb5c24-3e00-5e7d-af9c-2caa5063b2cd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7c2a831-facf-5fcc-90d0-c92cd81e88a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-141483" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=6$\n- **II.** $T$ is a bijection\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ and by the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not invertible. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $T$ is not invertible, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861 :topic/title "Linear Dependence and Independence"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9815692-a138-50b9-b4b5-53d1deb36416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451341} {:answer/id #uuid "13928f2f-cd46-56fa-8c97-9a173d41a9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13928f2f-cd46-56fa-8c97-9a173d41a9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451342} :answer-field/id #uuid "66207179-6782-51a7-8c9c-26b58946b0d4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452518} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bb7403c-1de8-5af0-a703-e840aa64edc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451343} {:answer/id #uuid "258dfd42-9353-508c-8008-3bf0dbe86ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bb7403c-1de8-5af0-a703-e840aa64edc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451343} :answer-field/id #uuid "95bc3e49-a85f-5996-a916-5f5842714a44" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452519} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba72e82-49bf-51a3-b88b-521926f583b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451345} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc155c3-cd22-58ba-9542-04ba60e1df40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcc155c3-cd22-58ba-9542-04ba60e1df40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451346} :answer-field/id #uuid "9358e9ed-4724-59aa-b3e0-47a55115aa88" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd19f076-6276-574d-a171-58c6f862f950" :question/math-academy-id "q-157239" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-2-3 \\\\ -10+1+9 \\\\ -5-1+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=5,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638 :topic/id #uuid "0748b9c2-fae7-5522-9655-db9e6f1d50c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1347 :topic/title "Probability Density Functions of Continuous Random Variables"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e1d6f24-a734-5128-a639-c1475cde6fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468948} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7860f7-ca02-5b26-93cd-e1d2dc5a27bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d7860f7-ca02-5b26-93cd-e1d2dc5a27bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468949} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7bd8e5b-c419-58d8-8e0b-2f980eaf8008" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470086} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8548247-62d3-5ed6-bab0-2b00d2dcb938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468950} {:answer/id #uuid "67063cac-3b9f-58f1-aab2-39dcbf47d7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67063cac-3b9f-58f1-aab2-39dcbf47d7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468951} :answer-field/id #uuid "db32c9da-914c-5153-b4eb-d5045aadd3eb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cad3423d-6b04-59bc-92d3-62454e0fb3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468952} {:answer/id #uuid "4381ac26-59ea-585a-9869-2e15c32a82df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4381ac26-59ea-585a-9869-2e15c32a82df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468953} :answer-field/id #uuid "869816d6-1110-5146-a195-04d224b888b5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef54c03e-38cc-58aa-92b0-b3a91ad36505" :question/math-academy-id "q-158422" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f955ee719a4e69f0d09c678f0a74023f137efd7b5ae36d49b98706156384173.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{0}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049519 :topic/id #uuid "fc01ef76-1fef-5e93-ace1-aec0dfa05f4a" :topic/math-academy-id 1989 :topic/title "Introduction to Recurrence Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2c8226e-1347-5559-9fb2-0649cc9fd255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468966} {:answer/id #uuid "0df0356f-0224-5c52-b4d6-276ba0c0b0b6" :answer/type {:db/i